{"Channel": {"name": "bug-georgebrown-ForecastForce", "channelID": "ch-force-42e754"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_8a3b36d4", "timestamp": "2026-11-02T03:30:00", "text": "The issue was titled 'Data Normalization Conflict' and was causing inconsistent model performance due to conflicting normalization techniques being applied simultaneously.", "utterranceID": "20261101-1-62bbe"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20261101-1-62bbe"}
{"Channel": {"name": "bug-georgebrown-ForecastForce", "channelID": "ch-force-42e754"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_8a3b36d4", "timestamp": "2026-11-02T03:34:00", "text": "The root cause was a bug in the data normalization step, where both min-max scaling and z-score normalization were being applied to the input data at the same time.", "utterranceID": "20261101-2-9951a"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20261101-2-9951a"}
{"Channel": {"name": "bug-georgebrown-ForecastForce", "channelID": "ch-force-42e754"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_8a3b36d4", "timestamp": "2026-11-02T03:37:00", "text": "I've fixed it by ensuring that only one normalization technique is applied based on a configuration setting. This should prevent any further conflicts. You can check the details in the PR here: https://github.com/salesforce/ForecastForce/pull/37", "utterranceID": "20261101-3-b5c78"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20261101-3-b5c78"}
{"Channel": {"name": "bug-georgebrown-ForecastForce", "channelID": "ch-force-42e754"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_86044ae5", "timestamp": "2026-11-02T03:38:00", "text": "Great work, @eid_8a3b36d4! Thanks for resolving this so quickly. I'll review the changes in the PR.", "utterranceID": "20261101-4-231f6"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20261101-4-231f6"}
