{"Channel": {"name": "develop-juliadavis-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-38c82b"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "slack_admin_bot", "timestamp": "2026-09-18T21:35:00", "text": "@eid_48e149b5 joined develop-juliadavis-PitchForce.", "utterranceID": "20260918-64-9ccbd"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260918-64-9ccbd"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-juliadavis-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-38c82b"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_48e149b5", "timestamp": "2026-09-19T02:26:00", "text": "Hi team, I came across some interesting open-source projects that have implemented similar backward compatibility features. Thought it might be useful to discuss them as we work on our PR. 😊", "utterranceID": "20260918-0-9d06a"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260918-0-9d06a"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-juliadavis-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-38c82b"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_48e149b5", "timestamp": "2026-09-19T02:29:00", "text": "First up, there's a PR from Django: [Backward Compatibility Layer for ORM Queries](https://github.com/django/django/pull/3625). It ensures existing ORM queries remain functional by translating them to the new query syntax. Sounds pretty relevant to what we're doing!", "utterranceID": "20260918-1-bc7bd"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260918-1-bc7bd"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-juliadavis-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-38c82b"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_8b67a68f", "timestamp": "2026-09-19T02:31:00", "text": "That's interesting, Alice! Django's approach could definitely offer some insights. Their ORM is quite robust, and maintaining backward compatibility there is no small feat. We might be able to borrow some ideas on how they handle query translation. 👍", "utterranceID": "20260918-2-a71a3"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260918-2-a71a3"}

