{"Channel": {"name": "develop-hannahmiller-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-42f139"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_17e55125", "timestamp": "2026-08-29T15:55:00", "text": "Sounds great, Julia! What do you have for us? 🤔", "utterranceID": "20260829-1-3a220"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260829-1-3a220"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-hannahmiller-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-42f139"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_a8a4fdcb", "timestamp": "2026-08-29T15:59:00", "text": "First up, we have a feature from Apache Kafka: Schema Registry Integration. This PR (https://github.com/apache/kafka/pull/3607) integrates a schema registry to manage and enforce data schemas for Kafka topics. It enhances data consistency and compatibility. Thoughts, @eid_73ca2844?", "utterranceID": "20260829-2-7e044"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260829-2-7e044"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-hannahmiller-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-42f139"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_73ca2844", "timestamp": "2026-08-29T16:04:00", "text": "Thanks, Julia! This is quite relevant. Ensuring data consistency is crucial, especially as we scale. We might not need a full schema registry, but the concept of enforcing data schemas could be adapted for our NoSQL setup. 👍", "utterranceID": "20260829-3-16f06"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260829-3-16f06"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-hannahmiller-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-42f139"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_8df92d08", "timestamp": "2026-08-29T16:09:00", "text": "I agree with David. Schema management could help us maintain data integrity across diverse customer profiles. We should definitely consider this approach. 😊", "utterranceID": "20260829-4-2f8f7"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260829-4-2f8f7"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-hannahmiller-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-42f139"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_a8a4fdcb", "timestamp": "2026-08-29T16:12:00", "text": "Great points! Next, let's look at ElasticSearch's GraphQL Query Support (https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/pull/3608). This feature allows users to use GraphQL syntax for more flexible and efficient data retrieval. What do you think, Emma?", "utterranceID": "20260829-5-e1cd1"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260829-5-e1cd1"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-hannahmiller-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-42f139"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_17e55125", "timestamp": "2026-08-29T16:15:00", "text": "GraphQL support is super interesting! It could offer our users a more intuitive way to query data. However, we need to evaluate if the added complexity is worth it for our use case. 🤔", "utterranceID": "20260829-6-fe971"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260829-6-fe971"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-hannahmiller-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-42f139"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_73ca2844", "timestamp": "2026-08-29T16:17:00", "text": "Agreed, Emma. While GraphQL is powerful, we should weigh its benefits against the learning curve for our team and users. Maybe we can explore a simpler query language first? 🤷‍♂️", "utterranceID": "20260829-7-53f26"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260829-7-53f26"}

