{"Channel": {"name": "develop-juliadavis-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-38c82b"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_c5bce3e8", "timestamp": "2026-09-19T02:36:00", "text": "Agreed, Charlie. Django's ORM is a good reference point. I wonder how they manage performance with the translation layer. 🤔", "utterranceID": "20260918-3-0f04b"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260918-3-0f04b"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-juliadavis-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-38c82b"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_48e149b5", "timestamp": "2026-09-19T02:40:00", "text": "Next, there's Kubernetes: [Legacy API Compatibility Layer](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/3626). They implemented a compatibility layer to support legacy API calls while transitioning to a new API version. This seems quite aligned with our REST to GraphQL translation.", "utterranceID": "20260918-4-5cd1f"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260918-4-5cd1f"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-juliadavis-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-38c82b"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_c5bce3e8", "timestamp": "2026-09-19T02:45:00", "text": "Kubernetes is always a great example for handling large-scale transitions. Their approach to API versioning and compatibility could be really beneficial for us. We should definitely look into how they manage deprecation warnings and user notifications. @eid_48e149b5, maybe you could dive deeper into this one?", "utterranceID": "20260918-5-ea785"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260918-5-ea785"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-juliadavis-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-38c82b"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_48e149b5", "timestamp": "2026-09-19T02:46:00", "text": "Sure thing, Fiona! I'll take a closer look at how Kubernetes handles these transitions and see what we can adapt. 🚀", "utterranceID": "20260918-6-1123c"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260918-6-1123c"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-juliadavis-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-38c82b"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_48e149b5", "timestamp": "2026-09-19T02:51:00", "text": "Lastly, there's React: [Legacy Component Compatibility Mode](https://github.com/facebook/react/pull/3627). They introduced a compatibility mode to ensure older components work with the latest version. It's more UI-focused, but the concept of maintaining legacy support is similar.", "utterranceID": "20260918-7-f639c"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260918-7-f639c"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-juliadavis-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-38c82b"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_8b67a68f", "timestamp": "2026-09-19T02:56:00", "text": "React's approach is interesting, especially since they focus on UI components. While it's a bit different from our API focus, their strategy for toggling compatibility modes could be useful. Maybe we can implement something similar for our API endpoints. 🤔", "utterranceID": "20260918-8-f8e9c"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260918-8-f8e9c"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-juliadavis-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-38c82b"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_c5bce3e8", "timestamp": "2026-09-19T02:59:00", "text": "Good point, Charlie. It might be worth considering a toggle feature for our compatibility layer. Let's keep that in mind as we move forward.", "utterranceID": "20260918-9-fdab7"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260918-9-fdab7"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-juliadavis-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-38c82b"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_48e149b5", "timestamp": "2026-09-19T03:01:00", "text": "Great discussion, team! I'll start by looking into the Kubernetes PR in more detail and see what we can apply to our project. Thanks for the input, everyone! 🙌", "utterranceID": "20260918-10-0b1db"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260918-10-0b1db"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-juliadavis-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-38c82b"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_48e149b5", "timestamp": "2026-09-21T13:12:00", "text": "Hi team, please check my PR for the backward compatibility layer: https://github.com/salesforce/pAIX/pull/15. This ensures our existing REST API endpoints continue to function by translating REST requests to GraphQL queries. Let me know your thoughts! 😊", "utterranceID": "20260925-0-81216"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260925-0-81216"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-juliadavis-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-38c82b"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_8b67a68f", "timestamp": "2026-09-21T13:16:00", "text": "Hey @eid_48e149b5, thanks for sharing! I'll take a look at it now. Just to confirm, this should mean no changes needed on the client side, right?", "utterranceID": "20260925-1-fd655"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260925-1-fd655"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-juliadavis-PitchForce", "channelID": "ch--paix-38c82b"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_48e149b5", "timestamp": "2026-09-21T13:19:00", "text": "Exactly, Charlie! The client code remains untouched. The compatibility layer handles everything under the hood. 👍", "utterranceID": "20260925-2-ddaef"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260925-2-ddaef"}

